Package: leandro-guest-dkms
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: amd64
Maintainer: Silas Müller <github@silasmueller.de>
Depends: dkms, build-essential, kmod
Recommends: libnvidia-compute-@BRANCH@ (>= @DRIVER@), libnvidia-compute-@BRANCH@ (<< @DRIVER@.0), nvidia-utils-@BRANCH@ (>= @DRIVER@), nvidia-utils-@BRANCH@ (<< @DRIVER@.0)
Suggests: libnvidia-gl-@BRANCH@ (>= @DRIVER@), libnvidia-gl-@BRANCH@ (<< @DRIVER@.0), libnvidia-extra-@BRANCH@ (>= @DRIVER@), libnvidia-extra-@BRANCH@ (<< @DRIVER@.0), libnvidia-encode-@BRANCH@ (>= @DRIVER@), libnvidia-encode-@BRANCH@ (<< @DRIVER@.0)
Conflicts: nvidia-dkms-@BRANCH@, nvidia-dkms-@BRANCH@-open, nvidia-dkms-@BRANCH@-server, nvidia-dkms-@BRANCH@-server-open
Section: kernel
Priority: optional
Homepage: https://github.com/UniStuttgart-IKR/Leandro
Description: Leandro guest driver: GPU paravirtualisation over virtio-nvrm (DKMS)
 The kernel side of a Leandro guest, built for the running kernel by dkms:
 nvrm_nodes and virtio_nvrm, and NVIDIA's open nvidia-modeset and nvidia-drm
 at driver @DRIVER@, linked against virtio_nvrm instead of nvidia.ko.
 .
 NVIDIA's userspace is NOT included and must be exactly version @DRIVER@,
 the host driver's; /etc/leandro/params must be the host's
 /proc/driver/nvidia/params.
 .
 A desktop guest also needs the virtual display switched on in
 /etc/modprobe.d/leandro-display.conf and NVIDIA's graphics userspace at the
 same version (Suggests).
